-- Passage rg9-literary-structure-pov-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ab4dabcd-6304-5dc8-a039-4a83aae55836', 'rg9-literary-structure-pov-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Obvious Move$q$, $q$The clock beside the board read four minutes for Priya and eleven for her opponent. Around them, the gym at Eastbrook High had gone quiet in the way only a room full of chess players can, with sixty people pretending not to watch one table. Priya kept her hands folded in her lap. Her knight could take the bishop on the next move. It was the obvious move. It was the move she wanted to make.

Last spring, at this same tournament, she had sat across from the same boy, Daniel Mwangi, in almost the same position. She remembered how fast her heart had been going and how quickly she had grabbed his bishop, pleased with herself, already picturing the trophy. Daniel had paused, nodded once, and then moved his rook to a square she had not even looked at. Three moves later her king had nowhere to go. She had shaken his hand and walked out to the parking lot without her coat.

Now she looked at the bishop again. It sat there, unprotected, almost polite. She let her eyes travel past it to the rook in the corner, then to the empty square beside her king. One second. Two. The clock ticked down to three minutes and forty seconds. Somewhere in the bleachers, a chair scraped.

She moved her king one square to the left.

Daniel looked up at her for the first time all afternoon. He studied the board, and then he studied her, and then he smiled the small, tired smile of someone whose plan has just been read aloud. He reached for his rook, stopped, and put his hand back on the table.

Priya pressed the clock and, finally, let herself breathe.$q$, 'draft')
