-- Passage rg9-literary-structure-pov-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('878522c9-b6f8-5d26-9a4e-264b65f9fdcb', 'rg9-literary-structure-pov-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Parent Night$q$, $q$Mr. Halvorsen speaks fast when he is nervous, and tonight he is nervous, because my mother is sitting very straight in a chair built for a ninth grader and looking at him as if he were a weather report she does not trust.

"Linh is a strong writer," he says. "But she has missed four homework assignments in October, and her last quiz was a fifty-two."

My mother turns to me. In our family, I have been the translator since I was eight. I have translated electric bills, a lease, a conversation with a plumber about something called a P-trap, and once, a doctor explaining my own ear infection to my mother while I held a tissue to my ear.

"He says I am a strong writer," I tell her in Vietnamese. "And he says October has been a busy month."

Both of these things are true. My mother nods slowly. She asks what else. I say he is pleased with my effort in class, which he did say last week, to someone.

Mr. Halvorsen waits. He does not speak Vietnamese, but he can count, and my answer had fewer words than his. He slides a printed grade report across the desk, toward my mother, not toward me.

My mother cannot read all the comments, but she can read numbers. She finds the 52 with one finger and rests it there. She does not look at Mr. Halvorsen. She looks at me, and she says, in Vietnamese, very gently, "Translate it again."

So I do. This time, I use all the words.$q$, 'draft')
