-- Passage rg9-literary-structure-pov-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('bc03d963-336b-5c89-9d5d-7385b0af5a55', 'rg9-literary-structure-pov-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Crow and the Weathervane$q$, $q$On the roof of a barn stood an iron weathervane shaped like a rooster, and every morning a young crow named Ash landed on its tail.

The first morning, the wind came from the west, and the rooster swung to face it just as Ash settled her feet. Ash was astonished. She shifted her weight to the left, and a moment later a gust from the south turned the rooster again. "So," she said to herself, "I am the one who steers it."

From then on, Ash took her duty seriously. She arrived before sunrise. She leaned this way and that, and the rooster turned, and the farmer below looked up at it and decided whether to cut his hay or wait. Ash saw him looking and felt the importance of her work in every feather.

One week she caught a cold and stayed in her nest for three days. She worried the whole time. Who would turn the rooster? Would the farmer cut his hay just before a storm? On the fourth morning she flew to the barn as fast as her stiff wings allowed, expecting ruin.

The hay stood in neat rows, dry and sweet. The rooster faced east, steady in a calm breeze. An old pigeon on the gutter watched her land.

"You were missed," the pigeon said kindly. Ash puffed up her chest. Then the pigeon added, "By me."

Ash looked at the rooster for a long time. Then she leaned to the left, very slightly, just to check. The rooster did not move.

The next morning she came back anyway, and this time she sat beside the pigeon.

Moral: The world may turn without our help, but our friends still notice when we are gone.$q$, 'draft')
