-- Passage rg9-literary-structure-pov-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2b0c2234-33df-5024-85a0-9bf1538354e1', 'rg9-literary-structure-pov-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Ridge Count$q$, $q$September 14. Arrived at the lookout at 7:40. Wind from the northeast, light. Clipboard, binoculars, thermos. Counted 0 broad-winged hawks by noon. A man with a camera asked if I was lost. I told him I was working. He did not believe me, which is fair, because I am fourteen and my binoculars are older than I am.

September 15. Same wind. Counted 3 broad-wings and 1 sharp-shinned hawk. I think. The sharp-shinned could have been a pigeon. Wrote it down with a question mark, the way the instructions on the inside cover say to. The handwriting on the inside cover is very neat and very small.

September 16. Rain. Did not count. Called home instead and read the numbers so far out loud. There was a long pause on the other end, and then a voice said, "A question mark is honest. Keep it."

September 17. Clear and cold, with a strong wind out of the north. At 10:15 the whole sky started moving. I could not count fast enough, so I did what the cover says: count ten birds, then estimate the rest in blocks of ten. Hawks rose in slow circles above the ridge, hundreds at a time, then slid south one after another like beads off a string. By 2:00 the total was 1,284. My hands were shaking so much that the numbers look like someone else wrote them. The man with the camera came back and asked who taught me to count like that.

September 18. Brought the notebook to Grandpa's apartment. His knee was still propped on the footstool, the way it has been since August, so the ridge will have to wait until next year for him. He ran his finger down the columns the way he used to read a bus schedule, slowly, checking everything. He stopped at 1,284 and tapped it twice.

"Next year," he said, "you sign the cover too."$q$, 'draft')
