-- Passage rg9-literary-structure-pov-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f4a6e09b-cf9f-5919-9624-b868b7223bd4', 'rg9-literary-structure-pov-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Empty Hollow$q$, $q$On Saturday mornings, Rafael Ortiz volunteered at the Fenwick County Natural History Museum, which mostly meant answering questions about the mastodon and reminding people not to lean on the glass. At 10:05, he noticed that the third hollow in the trilobite tray was empty.

There were supposed to be twelve trilobites, each resting in a shaped hollow in gray foam. Rafael counted twice. Eleven. The label for number three still read "Trilobite, 500 million years old," above nothing.

He thought immediately of the boy in the red sweatshirt. The boy had been at the case at 9:40, pressing his nose against the glass and asking how anyone knew how old a rock was. When Rafael answered, the boy had nodded seriously and written something in a small notebook. Then he had wandered toward the gift shop with his hands in his pockets.

Rafael checked the lock on the case. It was closed. He checked the gift shop. The boy was gone.

He was halfway through writing an incident report, his pen pressing hard enough to dent the page, when Dr. Amadi came down the stairs holding a small padded box.

"Sorry," she said. "I borrowed number three for the website photos. I did not mean to leave you a puzzle." She slid the trilobite back into its hollow. Then she paused and picked up a folded square of notebook paper from the shelf below the case, where the visitor comment cards were kept.

It said, in careful pencil: "Dear museum, number three is missing. I did not take it. I hope it is okay. I counted twice."

Rafael looked at the gift shop door for a long moment, and then he tore up his incident report.$q$, 'draft')
