   $q$Why does the author return to the present ("Right now") in the final paragraph after the flash-forward?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To leave the future unresolved and end on a warm moment that shows he belongs but is still learning, like a song everyone else knows","is_correct":true},{"text":"To prove that he will choose the name Ben at college","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"To shift the narration into his grandmother's point of view","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-point-of-view"},{"text":"To correct a mistake in the flash-forward about the college orientation","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To leave the future unresolved and end on a warm moment that shows he belongs but is still learning, like a song everyone else knows$q$, $q$Returning to the porch grounds the story in the present: he is laughing with his grandmother yet does not know the song "everyone else knows," so his place between cultures stays open. The ending never reveals which name he chooses.$q$, 'draft', '23ce4e8e-1ef2-5e5a-9ca0-c6c80479b761')
